2026-06-30 15:19:24,021 - openai._base_client - DEBUG - Request options: {'method': 'post', 'url': '/chat/completions', 'timeout': 300, 'files': None, 'idempotency_key': 'stainless-python-retry-9f821760-d73d-4d08-96f5-b2cdf82dc3ea', 'security': {'bearer_auth': True}, 'content': None, 'json_data': {'messages': [{'role': 'system', 'content': 'You are an expert proteomics assistant. Return valid JSON only.'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': 'You are a proteomics expert analyzing crosslinking mass spectrometry (XL-MS) datasets.\n\nYour task is to cluster raw spectral files into biologically meaningful groups.\n\nA cluster should represent a unique combination of:\n1. Biological / experimental condition\n   - Examples: treatment, control, carbon source, growth medium, cell state, tissue, disease state, time point, perturbation, purified complex, recombinant standard set.\n2. Source organism of the proteome being analyzed\n   - Return the full scientific name.\n   - Use the organism of the biological sample or proteome of interest, not necessarily the expression host.\n   - Example: if human proteins are expressed in E. coli and analyzed as human recombinant standards, the source organism is Homo sapiens.\n   - If the dataset intentionally contains multiple source organisms, create separate clusters by organism when possible.\n3. Crosslinking reagent\n   - Examples: BS3, DSS, DSSO, DSBU, formaldehyde.\n   - If multiple crosslinkers are used, create separate clusters by reagent when possible.\n\nDo NOT split clusters by:\n- fraction number\n- LC fraction\n- technical replicate\n- biological replicate ID\n- injection number\n- file numbering\n- enrichment fraction\n- instrument run order\n- search batch\n- calibration/QC files\n\nReplicates, fractions, and repeated injections from the same biological condition should be grouped together.\n\n---\n\nINPUTS\n\nRaw filenames:\nV_1.raw\nV_2.raw\nV_3.raw\nV_4.raw\nV_5.raw\nV_6.raw\n\nPublication text and PRIDE metadata:\n[TITLE]\nA direct interplay between the co-chaperones Hop and p23 regulates cycle progression and client folding in the Hsp70/Hsp90 chaperone machineries\n\n[ABSTRACT]\nFolding of stringent clients requires transfer from Hsp70 to Hsp90. The co-chaperone Hop physically connects the chaperone machineries. Here we define its role from the remodeling of Hsp70/40-client complexes to the mechanism of client transfer and the conformational switching from stalled to active client-processing states of Hsp90. We show that Hsp70 together with Hsp40 completely unfolds a stringent client, the glucocorticoid receptor ligand binding domain (GR-LBD) in large assemblies. Hop remodels these for efficient transfer onto Hsp90. As p23 enters, Hsp70 leaves the complex via switching between binding sites in Hop. To proceed to client folding, current concepts assume that Hop dissociates and the co-chaperone p23 stabilizes the Hsp90 closed state. In contrast, we show that p23 directly interacts with Hop, relieves the stalling Hsp90-Hop interaction and closes Hsp90. This reaction allows folding of the client and is thus the key regulatory step for the progression of the chaperone cycle. To study the interaction between the different proteins, especially p23 and the DP2 domain of Hop, we performed crosslinking-MS.\n\n[METHODS]\n10 µM GR-LBD were incubated with 15 µM Hsp70, 3 µM Hsp40 and 5 mM ATP for 1 h at 25°C. Afterwards, 15 µM Hop or TPR2A2B-DP2 and 20 µM each of Hsp90 and p23 were added and further incubated for 1 h at 25°C to allow Hop-Hsp90-GR-LBD-p23 complex formation. This was followed by the addition 4 µl of freshly prepared 30 mM disuccinimidyl glutarate (ThermoFisher Scientific; in DMSO) and the proteins were crosslinked for 1 h at 25 °C. The reaction was stopped by the addition of 6 µl 1 M Tris/HCl (pH 7.4). To denature the crosslinked proteins, 29.5 µl 6 M Urea, 50 mM Tris/HCl (pH 7.4) and 2.7 µl 100 mM DTT were added and the samples were incubated for 30 min at 56 °C. To alkylate the proteins, 6.3 µl 100 mM iodoacetamide (IAA) were added after the samples were cooled back to RT and they were incubated for 20 min at RT in the dark. The proteins were digested with 1 µl sequencing grade Trypsin (500 ng; Promega) over night at 37 °C. The digest was stopped by the addition of 1 µl formic acid (FA). The samples were desalted with double C18 layer stage tips. After equilibrating the tips with 70 µl methanol they were washed three times with 70 µl 0.5 % FA by applying mild centrifugation steps (960 x g). The peptides were applied onto the stage tips, washed three times with 70 µl 0.5 % FA and eluted with two times 30 µl 80 % ACN, 0.5 % FA (960 x g). Subsequently, the elution buffer was evaporated in a speed vacuum concentrator (Eppendorf). For the MS/MS measurement, the peptides were dissolved in 25 µl 0.5 % (v/v) FA and incubated for 15 min in an ultrasonic bath at RT. The peptide solutions were filtered with centrifugal filters (0.22 µM; Merck; 1 min at 7,000 x g) and transferred into Chromacol vials (ThermoFisher Scientific). Measurement MS/MS measurements were performed on an Orbitrap Fusion instrument coupled to an Ultimate3000 Nano-HPLC via an electrospray easy source (ThermoFisher Scientific). The peptide solutions were loaded on a 2 cm PepMap RSLC C18 trap column (particles 3 µm, 100 Å, inner diameter 75 µm, ThermoFisher Scientific) with 0.1 % TFA and separated on a 50 cm PepMap RSLC C18 column (particles 2 µm, 100 Å, inner diameter 75 µm, ThermoFisher Scientific) constantly held at 40 °C. Elution took place with a gradient from 5 to 35 % ACN, 0.1 % FA during 35 min at a flow rate of 0.4 µl/min (7 min 5 % ACN, 30 min to 28 % ACN, 5 min to 35 % ACN, 0.1 min to 90 % ACN, 10 min wash at 90 % ACN, 10 min equilibration at 5 % ACN). Survey scans (m/z 300 - 1,500) with a resolution of 120,000 were acquired. The automatic gain control (AGC) target value was set to 2.0*e5 with a maximum injection time of 80 ms. The most intense ions of charge states 2-12 were selected for HCD fragmentation and the collision energy was set to 30 %. The maximum injection time was set to 100 ms in the ion trap and the AGC target value was set to 5.0*e4. Inject ions for all available parallelizable time was allowed. Dynamic exclusion of sequenced peptides was set to 60 s. Data were acquired using Xcalibur software version 3.0sp2 (ThermoFisher Scientific).\n\n\nPreviously extracted organism(s) and NCBI taxid(s):\nSaccharomyces cerevisiae (baker\'s yeast); Homo sapiens (human)\n\n---\n\nTASKS\n\n1. Identify the distinct biological/experimental conditions described by the publication, PRIDE metadata, and filenames.\n\n2. Assign every raw file to exactly one cluster.\n\n3. Define clusters using the smallest number of biologically meaningful groups. Do not over-cluster based only on replicate, fraction, or run-number differences.\n\n4. For each cluster, report:\n   - cluster_id: "cluster_1", "cluster_2", etc.\n   - cluster_label: short machine-readable label using lowercase words separated by underscores\n   - description: concise human-readable summary of the condition\n   - organism: full scientific name of the proteome/sample organism\n   - taxid: NCBI Taxonomy CURIE, e.g. "NCBITaxon:9606"; use null if unknown\n   - crosslinker: crosslinking reagent; use null if unknown\n   - biological_condition: short phrase describing the treatment/control/state\n   - files: list of raw filenames assigned to this cluster\n   - confidence: "high", "medium", or "low"\n   - evidence_quote: exact sentence or short phrase from the publication, PRIDE metadata, or filename pattern that supports this cluster assignment\n   - reasoning: brief explanation of why these files belong together\n\n5. Assign files that cannot be confidently interpreted to cluster_0 with:\n   - cluster_label: "unassigned"\n   - confidence: "low"\n   - evidence_quote: null\n   - reasoning: explain why assignment was not possible\n\n6. QC, calibrant, blank, wash, or test files should be assigned to cluster_0 unless the publication clearly states they are part of the biological experiment.\n\n7. If all biological files appear to come from one condition, create only cluster_1 plus cluster_0 if there are unassigned/QC files.\n\n8. Confidence guidelines:\n   - "high": filenames and publication/metadata agree clearly\n   - "medium": assignment is likely but partially inferred from filenames or incomplete metadata\n   - "low": weak evidence, ambiguous naming, missing metadata, or conflicting information\n\n9. If publication text and filenames conflict, prioritize the publication/PRIDE metadata, but mention the conflict in reasoning.\n\n10. Return ONLY valid JSON. Do not include markdown, comments, or explanatory text outside the JSON.\n\n---\n\nOUTPUT JSON STRUCTURE\n\n{\n  "clusters": [\n    {\n      "cluster_id": "cluster_1",\n      "cluster_label": "yeast_glucose_bs3",\n      "description": "Saccharomyces cerevisiae samples grown in glucose-rich medium and crosslinked with BS3.",\n      "organism": "Saccharomyces cerevisiae",\n      "taxid": "NCBITaxon:4932",\n      "crosslinker": "BS3",\n      "biological_condition": "glucose-rich medium",\n      "files": [\n        "YeastGlu_R1.raw",\n        "YeastGlu_R2.raw",\n        "YeastGlu_fraction_01.raw"\n      ],\n      "confidence": "high",\n      "evidence_quote": "Yeast cells were cultured in glucose- or glycerol-containing medium and crosslinked with BS3.",\n      "reasoning": "The filenames contain Glu and the publication states that glucose-grown yeast samples were crosslinked with BS3."\n    },\n    {\n      "cluster_id": "cluster_0",\n      "cluster_label": "unassigned",\n      "description": "Files that could not be confidently assigned to a biological XL-MS condition.",\n      "organism": null,\n      "taxid": null,\n      "crosslinker": null,\n      "biological_condition": null,\n      "files": [\n        "calibrant.raw",\n        "QC.raw"\n      ],\n      "confidence": "low",\n      "evidence_quote": null,\n      "reasoning": "These files appear to be QC or calibration runs rather than biological samples."\n    }\n  ],\n  "summary": {\n    "n_input_files": 5,\n    "n_assigned_files": 3,\n    "n_unassigned_files": 2,\n    "n_biological_clusters": 1,\n    "overall_confidence": "high"\n  }\n}'}], 'model': 'gpt-5.4-nano-2026-03-17', 'store': True, 'temperature': 0.3}}
