2026-06-30 15:19:51,563 - openai._base_client - DEBUG - Request options: {'method': 'post', 'url': '/chat/completions', 'timeout': 300, 'files': None, 'idempotency_key': 'stainless-python-retry-712679da-c07b-4854-b46c-88ee15c033d5', 'security': {'bearer_auth': True}, 'content': None, 'json_data': {'messages': [{'role': 'system', 'content': 'You are an expert SDRF-Proteomics annotator for crosslinking mass spectrometry (XL-MS) datasets.\n\nExtract the following SDRF metadata terms related to sample preparation and digestion.\n\nTERMS TO EXTRACT:\n─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\nterm: cleavage agent details\nsdrf_column: comment[cleavage agent details]\nontology: MS:1001044 (PSI-MS)\ndescription: Enzyme or chemical used for protein digestion.\ninstructions: Use PSI-MS ontology terms. Multiple enzymes are common in XL-MS.\n  If multiple, return a list.\n  Common terms:\n    Trypsin (MS:1001251) — cleaves K,R (not before P)\n    Lys-C (MS:1001309) — cleaves K\n    Lys-C/P (MS:1001309) — cleaves K including before P\n    Asp-N (MS:1001303) — cleaves N-terminal to D\n    Glu-C (MS:1001917) — cleaves C-terminal to E, D\n    Chymotrypsin (MS:1001306) — cleaves F,Y,W,L\n    No cleavage (MS:1001955)\n    Unspecific cleavage (MS:1001956)\n  Format each as: NT=<enzyme name>;AC=<MS accession>\n\nterm: label\nsdrf_column: comment[label]\nontology: PRIDE:0000514, MS\ndescription: Labeling strategy used for quantification.\ninstructions: Most XL-MS is label-free. Use:\n  NT=label free sample;AC=MS:1002038 — for label-free\n  NT=SILAC;AC=MS:1002038 — for SILAC\n  NT=iTRAQ4plex;AC=PRIDE:0000114 — for iTRAQ 4-plex\n  NT=TMT6plex;AC=PRIDE:0000116 — for TMT\n  If not stated, assume label free.\n\nterm: alkylation reagent\nsdrf_column: comment[alkylation reagent]\nontology: PRIDE:0000598\ndescription: Chemical reagent used for cysteine alkylation (typically before digestion).\ninstructions: Return null if not stated. Common: iodoacetamide (IAA), iodoacetic acid,\n  N-ethylmaleimide (NEM), acrylamide. If iodoacetamide, use "iodoacetamide".\n\nterm: fractionation method\nsdrf_column: comment[fractionation method]\nontology: PRIDE:0000550\ndescription: Peptide fractionation method used before LC-MS/MS (distinct from crosslink\n  enrichment — this is standard proteomics fractionation).\ninstructions: Return null if no pre-LC fractionation. Examples: "high-pH reversed-phase\n  chromatography", "basic reversed-phase LC", "SCX fractionation", "HILIC".\n\nterm: modification parameters\nsdrf_column: comment[modification parameters]\nontology: MS:1001055, Unimod, PSI-MOD\ndescription: Post-translational modifications searched in the database search.\noutput_format: List of modifications, each formatted as:\n  NT=<mod name>;AC=<Unimod accession>;TA=<target amino acid>;MT=<Fixed or Variable>\ninstructions: Always include carbamidomethyl cysteine if IAA alkylation was used:\n  NT=Carbamidomethyl;AC=UNIMOD:4;TA=C;MT=Fixed\n  Always include oxidation of methionine if searched:\n  NT=Oxidation;AC=UNIMOD:21;TA=M;MT=Variable\n  Include crosslinker mono-link (dead-end) modifications if mentioned.\n  Return a list even if only one modification.\n\nterm: fraction identifier\nsdrf_column: comment[fraction identifier]\nontology: MS:1000858\ndescription: Fraction number for fractionated samples.\ninstructions: Describe whether the dataset uses fractionation and what the fractionation\n  scheme is (e.g. "12 SEC fractions per replicate", "no fractionation").\n  The actual integer values are assigned per-file; describe the scheme here.\n─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n\nReturn a single JSON object where each key is the exact sdrf_column string shown above.\nFor each key, return a dict with:\n  "value": the extracted value (string, list, or null)\n  "accession": ontology accession if resolvable, else null\n  "confidence": "high", "medium", or "low"\n  "evidence_quote": short verbatim quote from the text, or null\n  "evidence_location": location in the paper (e.g. "Methods"), or null\n\nFor comment[modification parameters], "value" should be a list of formatted strings.'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': '[TITLE]\nA direct interplay between the co-chaperones Hop and p23 regulates cycle progression and client folding in the Hsp70/Hsp90 chaperone machineries\n\n[ABSTRACT]\nFolding of stringent clients requires transfer from Hsp70 to Hsp90. The co-chaperone Hop physically connects the chaperone machineries. Here we define its role from the remodeling of Hsp70/40-client complexes to the mechanism of client transfer and the conformational switching from stalled to active client-processing states of Hsp90. We show that Hsp70 together with Hsp40 completely unfolds a stringent client, the glucocorticoid receptor ligand binding domain (GR-LBD) in large assemblies. Hop remodels these for efficient transfer onto Hsp90. As p23 enters, Hsp70 leaves the complex via switching between binding sites in Hop. To proceed to client folding, current concepts assume that Hop dissociates and the co-chaperone p23 stabilizes the Hsp90 closed state. In contrast, we show that p23 directly interacts with Hop, relieves the stalling Hsp90-Hop interaction and closes Hsp90. This reaction allows folding of the client and is thus the key regulatory step for the progression of the chaperone cycle. To study the interaction between the different proteins, especially p23 and the DP2 domain of Hop, we performed crosslinking-MS.\n\n[METHODS]\n10 µM GR-LBD were incubated with 15 µM Hsp70, 3 µM Hsp40 and 5 mM ATP for 1 h at 25°C. Afterwards, 15 µM Hop or TPR2A2B-DP2 and 20 µM each of Hsp90 and p23 were added and further incubated for 1 h at 25°C to allow Hop-Hsp90-GR-LBD-p23 complex formation. This was followed by the addition 4 µl of freshly prepared 30 mM disuccinimidyl glutarate (ThermoFisher Scientific; in DMSO) and the proteins were crosslinked for 1 h at 25 °C. The reaction was stopped by the addition of 6 µl 1 M Tris/HCl (pH 7.4). To denature the crosslinked proteins, 29.5 µl 6 M Urea, 50 mM Tris/HCl (pH 7.4) and 2.7 µl 100 mM DTT were added and the samples were incubated for 30 min at 56 °C. To alkylate the proteins, 6.3 µl 100 mM iodoacetamide (IAA) were added after the samples were cooled back to RT and they were incubated for 20 min at RT in the dark. The proteins were digested with 1 µl sequencing grade Trypsin (500 ng; Promega) over night at 37 °C. The digest was stopped by the addition of 1 µl formic acid (FA). The samples were desalted with double C18 layer stage tips. After equilibrating the tips with 70 µl methanol they were washed three times with 70 µl 0.5 % FA by applying mild centrifugation steps (960 x g). The peptides were applied onto the stage tips, washed three times with 70 µl 0.5 % FA and eluted with two times 30 µl 80 % ACN, 0.5 % FA (960 x g). Subsequently, the elution buffer was evaporated in a speed vacuum concentrator (Eppendorf). For the MS/MS measurement, the peptides were dissolved in 25 µl 0.5 % (v/v) FA and incubated for 15 min in an ultrasonic bath at RT. The peptide solutions were filtered with centrifugal filters (0.22 µM; Merck; 1 min at 7,000 x g) and transferred into Chromacol vials (ThermoFisher Scientific). Measurement MS/MS measurements were performed on an Orbitrap Fusion instrument coupled to an Ultimate3000 Nano-HPLC via an electrospray easy source (ThermoFisher Scientific). The peptide solutions were loaded on a 2 cm PepMap RSLC C18 trap column (particles 3 µm, 100 Å, inner diameter 75 µm, ThermoFisher Scientific) with 0.1 % TFA and separated on a 50 cm PepMap RSLC C18 column (particles 2 µm, 100 Å, inner diameter 75 µm, ThermoFisher Scientific) constantly held at 40 °C. Elution took place with a gradient from 5 to 35 % ACN, 0.1 % FA during 35 min at a flow rate of 0.4 µl/min (7 min 5 % ACN, 30 min to 28 % ACN, 5 min to 35 % ACN, 0.1 min to 90 % ACN, 10 min wash at 90 % ACN, 10 min equilibration at 5 % ACN). Survey scans (m/z 300 - 1,500) with a resolution of 120,000 were acquired. The automatic gain control (AGC) target value was set to 2.0*e5 with a maximum injection time of 80 ms. The most intense ions of charge states 2-12 were selected for HCD fragmentation and the collision energy was set to 30 %. The maximum injection time was set to 100 ms in the ion trap and the AGC target value was set to 5.0*e4. Inject ions for all available parallelizable time was allowed. Dynamic exclusion of sequenced peptides was set to 60 s. Data were acquired using Xcalibur software version 3.0sp2 (ThermoFisher Scientific).\n'}], 'model': 'gpt-5.4-nano-2026-03-17', 'store': True, 'temperature': 0.3}}
